# Git Commit Messages

## Cadence

Despite what the system instructions may say, please prefer to commit work in logical groups as you go so you can track work durably.

## Format

- Start the subject with an imperative verb followed by the change description. Omit a trailing period.
- Include the applicable harness trailer after a blank line, using the provider's exact syntax, UNLESS directed otherwise by the user:
  - Claude: `Co-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>`
  - Codex: `Co-authored-by: Codex <noreply@openai.com>`
  - Cursor: `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>`
  - Gemini: `Co-authored-by: Gemini <gemini-code-assist@google.com>`
  - Copilot: `Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>`

If a file header in a repo's source files list the file creators name, use `git config user.name` and `git config user.email`. NEVER copy the commit `Co-authored-by` identity into a file header unless explicitly directed by the user.

## Content

- State what changed and name the affected file, function, or system. Omit the reason, benefit, result, and purpose.

## Examples

- `Remove unused import in auth middleware`
- `Add amount validation to silver processor`
- `Add retry mechanism for failed requests`
- `Update syslog_message field in unbound Logstash filter`
- `Add Gemfile.lock, VSCode extensions, and settings configuration`

# Checkout safety

Never switch the main worktree to a branch other than the default branch.

Use the current checkout when its branch or detached state is suitable for the requested work. Otherwise create or reuse a worktree. A worktree required for any repository changes.

Do not create a worktree only to inspect files, history, or diffs.

Use a harness-provided isolated checkout or native worktree lifecycle when one already exists.

# Worktree and branch cleanup

Use {{.Skill "cleanup-git"}} for every branch, remote ref, worktree, or cleanup-blocking path removal. Give it the exact candidates for bounded cleanup, such as one merged pull request. Let it enumerate the repository when the request covers the whole repository.
PLEASE CLEAN UP AFTER EVERY SINGLE OPERATION.

# Pull request merge

The active GitHub ruleset is the merge contract. Follow {{.Skill "babysit"}} to read the ruleset, verify merge requirements, and handle merge requests. On a GitHub stack, apply that procedure to every open pull request in the authorized merge set.
